WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.529
<v Examiner>Exam case 15.

2
00:00:02.529 --> 00:00:03.991
<v Examiner>Genetic diseases.

3
00:00:04.441 --> 00:00:09.248
<v Examiner>You're working in a rural GP clinic, alongside two families with genetic diseases.

4
00:00:09.248 --> 00:00:11.475
<v Examiner>The Harris whānau has cystic fibrosis.

5
00:00:11.475 --> 00:00:14.113
<v Examiner>The Anderson whānau has Huntington's disease.

6
00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:18.157
<v Examiner>You want to understand how these diseases pass from person to person,

7
00:00:18.157 --> 00:00:22.319
<v Examiner>and how they might affect any future children born into these families.

8
00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:26.237
One answered question, and four more on her two whānau charts,

9
00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:28.865
whose model answers are still hidden on Canvas.

10
00:00:28.865 --> 00:00:32.557
So you'll get the method for those: whatever the chart looks like,

11
00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:34.011
you'll be able to work it.

12
00:00:34.461 --> 00:00:35.076
<v Examiner>Six marks.

13
00:00:35.076 --> 00:00:39.874
<v Examiner>What are the modes of inheritance of Huntington's disease and cystic fibrosis?

14
00:00:39.874 --> 00:00:44.979
<v Examiner>Explain both: the chromosome type, the pattern of inheritance, and what this means.

15
00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.526
Cystic fibrosis: autosomal recessive, on chromosome seven.

16
00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:52.988
It skips generations, and people can be carriers.

17
00:00:52.988 --> 00:00:57.439
The affected person is little r little r: homozygous recessive.

18
00:00:57.889 --> 00:01:02.154
Huntington's disease: autosomal dominant, on chromosome four.

19
00:01:02.154 --> 00:01:05.998
It's expressed whenever the dominant allele is present,

20
00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:09.773
so in every generation someone is expected to have it.

21
00:01:09.773 --> 00:01:13.548
The affected person is big R little r, or big R big R.

22
00:01:13.998 --> 00:01:19.583
When she marked a student's answer to this in her session, she added one thing in red:

23
00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:22.960
the answer must name the affected person's genotype.

24
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.583
So always finish with it.

25
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:27.051
Little r little r for cystic fibrosis.

26
00:01:27.051 --> 00:01:30.233
Big R little r, or big R big R, for Huntington's.

27
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:32.892
<v Examiner>Now her two charts.

28
00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:36.965
<v Examiner>Her model answers aren't posted yet, so here's how to work them.

29
00:01:36.965 --> 00:01:39.956
<v Examiner>For the Harris whānau's chart, cystic fibrosis,

30
00:01:39.956 --> 00:01:43.075
<v Examiner>state the genotype and phenotype of every person.

31
00:01:43.525 --> 00:01:47.545
Cystic fibrosis is recessive, so start with the shaded people.

32
00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:50.333
Every affected person is little r little r.

33
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:54.872
Next, the parents of an affected child who aren't affected themselves:

34
00:01:54.872 --> 00:01:59.865
each of them must have given a little r, so they're carriers, big R little r.

35
00:01:59.865 --> 00:02:04.858
Then the unaffected children of two carriers: big R big R, or big R little r.

36
00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:08.165
You can't tell which from the chart, so write both.

37
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:11.342
For the phenotype, write affected, or unaffected.

38
00:02:11.342 --> 00:02:13.741
To be precise, an unaffected carrier.

39
00:02:14.191 --> 00:02:16.872
<v Examiner>The two youngest sisters of the Harris whānau.

40
00:02:16.872 --> 00:02:20.602
<v Examiner>What's the chance they pass this condition on to their children,

41
00:02:20.602 --> 00:02:23.109
<v Examiner>and what circumstances would have to occur?

42
00:02:23.559 --> 00:02:28.983
The circumstance first: a child can only be affected if both parents pass on a little r.

43
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:32.866
So the sister has to carry a little r, and so does her partner.

44
00:02:32.866 --> 00:02:37.673
If a sister is a carrier and her partner is a carrier, it's the classic cross:

45
00:02:37.673 --> 00:02:42.789
twenty-five per cent affected, fifty per cent carriers, twenty-five per cent clear.

46
00:02:42.789 --> 00:02:47.782
If a sister is affected, little r little r, every child gets a little r from her,

47
00:02:47.782 --> 00:02:52.096
so with a carrier partner it's a fifty per cent chance for each child.

48
00:02:52.096 --> 00:02:56.164
And with a big R big R partner, none of the children are affected,

49
00:02:56.164 --> 00:02:57.829
though they'll be carriers.

50
00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:02.202
<v Examiner>For the Anderson whānau's chart, Huntington's,

51
00:03:02.202 --> 00:03:05.316
<v Examiner>state the genotype and phenotype of every person.

52
00:03:05.766 --> 00:03:09.314
Huntington's is dominant, so it's the other way round.

53
00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:15.162
On her chart the letters may be R and little r, or H and little h; the logic is the same.

54
00:03:15.162 --> 00:03:19.301
Every affected person is big H little h, or rarely big H big H.

55
00:03:19.301 --> 00:03:22.258
Every unaffected person is little h little h.

56
00:03:22.258 --> 00:03:24.229
There are no healthy carriers.

57
00:03:24.229 --> 00:03:27.974
And every affected person should have an affected parent.

58
00:03:28.424 --> 00:03:32.251
<v Examiner>The six cousins in the youngest generation of the Anderson whānau.

59
00:03:32.251 --> 00:03:35.613
<v Examiner>What's the chance they could pass it on to their children?

60
00:03:36.063 --> 00:03:37.385
Go cousin by cousin.

61
00:03:37.385 --> 00:03:41.680
An unaffected cousin is little h little h, so the chance is zero:

62
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.662
they have no big H to pass on.

63
00:03:43.662 --> 00:03:48.221
An affected cousin is big H little h, and with an unaffected partner,

64
00:03:48.221 --> 00:03:51.128
it's a fifty per cent chance for each child.

65
00:03:51.128 --> 00:03:55.225
One real-world catch: Huntington's usually shows in adulthood,

66
00:03:55.225 --> 00:03:57.868
so a young cousin might not show it yet.

67
00:03:57.868 --> 00:04:00.115
But on a chart, go by the shading.

68
00:04:00.565 --> 00:04:01.430
<v Examiner>Once more.

69
00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:02.122
<v Examiner>Case 15.

70
00:04:04.572 --> 00:04:10.187
Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive, on chromosome seven: it skips generations,

71
00:04:10.187 --> 00:04:14.638
there are carriers, and the affected person is little r little r.

72
00:04:14.638 --> 00:04:20.458
Huntington's is autosomal dominant, on chromosome four: it shows in every generation,

73
00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:24.429
and the affected person is big R little r, or big R big R.

74
00:04:24.429 --> 00:04:29.360
On a recessive chart, label the affected people little r little r first;

75
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.962
their unaffected parents are carriers.

76
00:04:31.962 --> 00:04:36.070
A child is affected only if both parents pass on a little r:

77
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.740
two carriers give twenty-five per cent.

78
00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:44.492
On a dominant chart, affected is big H little h and unaffected is little h little h.

79
00:04:44.492 --> 00:04:47.094
An unaffected cousin can't pass it on;

80
00:04:47.094 --> 00:04:51.203
an affected one has a fifty per cent chance with each child.
